lima/ppir: let more than one unit read ^texture in the texld's instruction

The Mali blob draws the Demo's tint quad (capture prog 40; the GLSL is
capture/demo/decoded/glsl/prog-40.frag:

  vec4 t = texture2D(u_tex, v_uv);
  gl_FragColor = vec4(t.rgb * u_gain, t.a);

) in ONE instruction, @0 of capture/demo/compare-26.2.6/prog-40.blob.pp.txt:

  load.v ^discard 0.xy, texld 0, load.u 1.x,
  mov.s1 $0.w ^texture.w, mul.s2 $0.xyz ^uniform.x ^texture.xyzz, sync, stop

The sampler pipeline register is read TWICE in the instruction that issues the
texld: ^texture.w by the float accumulator and ^texture.xyz by the result
combiner.

lima spent two instructions.  ppir_lower_texture() only turned the texture
load's dest into ^texture when the load had exactly one successor; with a
successor per use it fell back to copying ^texture into a work register, so the
texld needed an instruction of its own and every reader then addressed the copy:

  load.v ^discard.xy 0.xy, texld 0, mov.v1 $1 ^texture, sync
  load.u 0, mov.s1 $0.w $1.w, mul.s2 $0.xyz ^uniform.x $1.xyzx, stop

Allow the pipeline register whenever every reader is an ALU node of the same
block, and decide in node_to_instr: a pipeline register is read in the
instruction that writes it, so the texld can only join the readers if they all
landed in ONE instruction, which ppir_do_node_to_instr_try_insert() now checks
(it compared nothing before - a pipeline dest was asserted to have a single
reader).  When they did not, ppir_do_one_node_to_instr() inserts the copy
exactly as the lowering pass used to, now over every reader instead of the
first one; that fallback is what keeps the pass honest and it is the common
case (5 of the 10 shaders in beat-shader3/stress/glsl take it).
ppir_node_score()'s walk down a pipeline chain follows the first reader, since
every reader gives the same chain length.

Where the blob's decision lives: its PP backend is about 0x000d4120-0x000eb1f0
in libGLES_mali.so (r6p0-01rel1) and the fragment register allocator is the
function at 0x000e38a0, which reports "%s register allocation failed for
fragment shader." from 0x000e3be8.  I did not isolate its predicate; the
evidence for what the hardware allows is the blob's emitted code, which the
capture decodes in full.  Across the Demo, Games and Photos programs the blob
reads ^texture from the vec4 multiply ("mul.v0 ^texture.xyzz $0.xyzz"), the
float multiply ("mul.s0 $0.w ^uniform.w ^texture.w", 3 programs), the vec4
accumulator ("add.v1 $2 ^texture $0"), the float accumulator and the result
combiner (prog 40 above) - every ALU unit.  It also reads one pipeline register
from two units of a single instruction in 12 instructions, 10 of them with one
reader in the multiply stage and one in the accumulate/combine stage (e.g.
"mul.v0 $1.xyz ^const0.wwww $0.xyzz, mul.s2 $0.xyz ^texture.w ^const0.xyzz").
beat-shader3/pipecross.py counts them from any disassembly.

Counts (one limacompile process per program, PP instructions, base = Mesa
26.2.6 + the image's 0001-0007 then 0010-0015, then 0016 and 0017):
  Demo prog 40  (the tint quad, horizon)              2 -> 1  = the blob's 1
  Demo prog 16  (the tunnel's textured sprite)        3 -> 2  under the blob's 3
  reference UI prog 12  (Home's textured rounded rect)       12 -> 11
  Demo total 44 -> 42 (blob 45), words 270 -> 264; Home 33 -> 32
  Games, Photos and Clock unchanged; every GP count unchanged (the patch is
  ir/pp only); the other 65 of the 68 captured shaders disassemble byte for
  byte as before; no shader spills (0:0 spills:fills for all 68).

Predicted GPU time: fill-weighted against the blob's own per-job PP counter
(beat-shader3/fillest.py, cycles per instruction kept at the blob's) the Demo's
horizon scene goes from 13.38 ms of PP a frame to 13.24 = the blob's 13.24
(x1.01 -> x1.00); prog 40 alone is 0.285 -> 0.143 ms there (horizon.py).  Tunnel
gains more but less precisely, 12.33 ms -> an interval of 10.0..12.5, because
prog 16 sits inside the part of the counter the model cannot split.  Home's
prog 12 is 2 draws a frame, so under 0.1 ms.  No other scene moves.

Picture risk: BIT-IDENTICAL.  The patch changes no arithmetic.  In each of the
three programs every ALU unit keeps its opcode, its operand order, its swizzle
and its output modifier; the only change is the ADDRESS of one operand - the
work register that held a copy of the sampler result becomes the ^texture it was
copied from - plus the register numbers regalloc then picks and one fewer
instruction.  The copy that disappears was exact: ppir_node_insert_mov() builds
a 4-component mov with no modifier, no saturate and no negate/absolute, and
^texture and the work registers are the same fp16 format, so the register held
^texture bit for bit and reading ^texture under the reader's unchanged swizzle
delivers the same bits to the same unit.  Side by side, prog 40:

  before  mov.s1 $0.w $1.w,       mul.s2 $0.xyz ^uniform.x $1.xyzx
  after   mov.s1 $0.w ^texture.w, mul.s2 $0.xyz ^uniform.x ^texture.xyzx

Depends on one hardware behaviour, named because it is not an arithmetic risk:
the sampler pipeline register can be read by more than one ALU unit of the
instruction that issues the texld.  The blob's prog 40 proves two readers are
legal (float accumulator + combiner - that instruction is exactly what this
patch reproduces), and the blob reads ^texture from each of the five ALU units
somewhere in the captures.  What no captured blob program does is read ^texture
from a multiply unit and an accumulate unit at once, which lima's new prog 16
does ("mul.v0 ^texture.wwwx ^const0.xyzx, add.v1 $1.xyz ^v0 ^texture.xyzx").
Two things cover that: the blob reads ^const0 and ^uniform across those two
stages in 10 instructions, so it is a property of the operand network and not of
^texture; and the 0001-0015 build measured on the board at boot 478-1 already
emits the same shape for ^uniform - reference UI program 6, the polygon edge fill of the
Games, Clock and Home screens, has 4 instructions of
"mul.v0 ^uniform.xyxx $0.xyxx, sum2add.v1 $0.z ^v0.xyyy ^uniform.zzzz" (0014's
op 0x12 fuse) and renders.  A failure costs the instruction back rather than the
picture everywhere except that cross-stage case: if it does not hold, prog 16's
tunnel sprite colour is wrong.

Check on the board: render shots must be BYTE-IDENTICAL to the 0001-0017 build
in every Demo scene and on Home.  Watch horizon (prog 40) and the tunnel (prog
16) first; a wrong tunnel sprite with a correct horizon is exactly the
cross-stage read above, and dropping prog 16 then means restricting
ppir_texture_readers_can_pipeline() to readers in the accumulate and combine
slots, which keeps prog 40 and Home's prog 12.
